  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Response to Severe Tropical Storm Kristine</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 23 October 2024, Severe Tropical Storm Kristine hit the Philippines, particularly impacting the Bicol region. The region experienced violent and sustained winds, widespread flooding and landslides. As of 31 October, it has affected more than two million families or 7.9 million persons in 16 out of the 18 regions of the Philippines. Following the declaration of a State of Calamity in multiple provinces by the authorities, an immediate humanitarian response has been provided by the national and local government authorities, non-governmental organizations and partners. The International Organization for Migration is contributing to the response to the adverse effects of the Severe Tropical Storm Kristine. Assistance includes camp coordination and camp management technical assistance in evacuation centres and infrastructure rehabilitation or improvement as needed; water, sanitation and hygiene support in evacuation centres through installation of water tanks and transportation of water; multi-purpose cash assistance to allow the most vulnerable households to cater for their basic needs; shelter through the distribution of shelter repair kits and trainings; and mental health and psychosocial support.</narrative>
  </description>
